= Licorice Root

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Glycyrrhiza glabra_ L. (root/rhizome; the drug = _Liquiritiae radix_), family Fabaceae; the Chinese species is _Glycyrrhiza uralensis_ Fisch. _Glycyrrhiza_ is straight from Greek _γλυκύρριζα_ (_glykýrrhiza_) — _glykýs_ (γλυκύς, "sweet") + _rhíza_ (ῥίζα, "root") = "sweet root." _glabra_ = "smooth/hairless" (of the pods). The pharmacy term _liquiritia_ is a Vulgar-Latin corruption of _glycyrrhiza_, and that corruption is the source of the English word.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _γλυκύρριζα_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _glycyrrhiza_, _liquiritia_); _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "liquorice"). Species identity: _Glycyrrhiza glabra_ (Western/Indian officinal) vs. _G. uralensis_ (Chinese 甘草) per the respective pharmacopoeias.]

*English:* _Licorice_ / _liquorice_, from Old French _licoresse_ / Anglo-Norman _lycorys_, from Late Latin _liquiritia_ (the corrupted _glycyrrhiza_). So "licorice" and "Glycyrrhiza" are the same Greek word, one clean and one mangled through Latin.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "liquorice, n."); the _liquiritia_ < _glycyrrhiza_ corruption is discussed in the OED etymology and in Skeat, _An Etymological Dictionary of the English Language_.]

*Chinese:* 甘草 (gāncǎo), lit. "sweet herb" (甘 gān = sweet; 草 cǎo = herb/grass) — a direct semantic match to the Greek "sweet root." Honey-fried licorice is 炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo), a distinct prepared form.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典). The 甘草 / 炙甘草 distinction follows the standard materia medica; see Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Sanskrit:* यष्टिमधु (_yaṣṭimadhu_) — _yaṣṭi_ (यष्टि, "stick/rod") + _madhu_ (मधु, "honey/sweet") = "the sweet stick." Also यष्टीमधु (_yaṣṭīmadhu_), मधुक (_madhuka_, "the sweet one"), मधुयष्टि (_madhuyaṣṭi_). Greek, Chinese, and Sanskrit all name it "sweet root/stick/herb."#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _yaṣṭimadhu_, _madhuka_, _yaṣṭi_, _madhu_).]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Species: _G. uralensis_. One of the most-used drugs in the entire Chinese pharmacopoeia.#footnote[甘草 is listed among the superior class (上品) in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經); it appears in a very large fraction of formulas in Zhang Zhongjing's _Shanghan Lun_ (傷寒論). See Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009).]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平). Honey-fried 炙甘草 becomes warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* All twelve channels classically; principally Heart (心), Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).#footnote[Property, flavor, and channel data: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 甘草 entry; systematized in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- 補脾益氣 — tonifies Spleen and augments qi (esp. 炙甘草, for qi vacuity, palpitations, shortness of breath).
- 潤肺止咳 — moistens the Lung and stops cough.
- 清熱解毒 — clears heat and resolves toxicity (raw 生甘草, for sore throat, carbuncles, drug/food toxicity).
- 緩急止痛 — relaxes spasm/tension and stops pain (e.g. with peony in 芍藥甘草湯).
- *調和諸藥* — harmonizes the actions of all other medicinals; moderates harsh drugs, reconciles hot and cold, "escorts" the formula. This is licorice's signature role and the reason it appears in so many prescriptions — a conceptual cousin of the Ayurvedic _anupāna_ idea, though licorice buffers rather than amplifies.#footnote[Action list and the harmonizing role: _Bencao Gangmu_ 甘草 entry; see Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Cautions:* Sweet and moistening — can cause water retention and abdominal fullness/distension (中滿) with prolonged or high use; classically contraindicated in damp-excess with fullness and edema. The eighteen incompatibilities (十八反) name licorice as antagonistic to 甘遂 (gānsuì, _Euphorbia kansui_), 大戟 (dàjǐ), 芫花 (yuánhuā), and 海藻 (hǎizǎo, seaweed).#footnote[The water-retention / 中滿 caution: _Bencao Gangmu_ 甘草 entry and standard TCM dietetics. The 十八反 (Eighteen Incompatibilities) antagonist pairs are a classical mnemonic set; see Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., appendix on incompatibilities.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Species: _G. glabra_. A leading rasāyana drug.#footnote[Yaṣṭimadhu is included among the _jīvanīya_ and _sandhānīya_ groups in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4; full dravya treatment in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba), and Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — predominant; a slight tikta (bitter) sometimes noted.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous).

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of yaṣṭimadhu: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* Classically _cakṣuṣya_ (eye-benefiting), _varṇya_ (complexion-enhancing), _keśya_ (hair-promoting), and a premier _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ / _sandhānīya_ (wound-healing, tissue-uniting) and _medhya_ / _jīvanīya_ agent. The cooling–sweet–unctuous profile predicts the Pitta-pacifying demulcent action; its potency as a _rasāyana_ and _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ agent (and use as a mild _vamana_/emetic in larger dose) is treated at the prabhāva level.#footnote[The _cakṣuṣya_/_keśya_/_vraṇa-ropaṇa_/_rasāyana_ attributions: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; emetic (_vamana_) use in Caraka Saṃhitā, Kalpasthāna. On prabhāva as a category: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The "prabhāva-level" framing is interpretive — flagged as such.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Pitta (vāta-pitta-śāmaka); increases Kapha in excess/long use.#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 and 27; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Jīvanīya, Rasāyana_ — vitalizing, rejuvenative.
- _Vṛṣya_ — aphrodisiac; _Balya_ — strengthening.
- _Kāsahara, Svarya_ — relieves cough, improves/clears the voice (classic for hoarseness and throat).
- _Vraṇa-ropaṇa, Sandhānīya_ — wound-healing, tissue-knitting.
- _Cakṣuṣya, Varṇya, Keśya_ — good for eyes, complexion, hair.
- _Medhya_ — promotes intellect/memory.
- _Pittahara, Dāhahara_ — relieves Pitta and burning.
- _Mūtrala_ — mild diuretic; _Vamaka_ — emetic in larger dose (used in _vamana_ therapy).#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, and Caraka Saṃhitā (Sūtrasthāna 4 _jīvanīya/sandhānīya gaṇa_, Cikitsāsthāna respiratory and _kṣata-kṣīṇa_ chapters). Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional; M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional (doṣa) viruddha:* contraindicated in Kapha excess, _medo-roga_ (obesity/lipid disorders), edema (_śotha_), and _kledādhikya_ (excess fluid/dampness) — sweet–heavy–unctuous nature aggravates these.
- *(—) Named food-pairs:* few in the classical texts; yaṣṭimadhu is more often a _harmonizer/anupāna_ than a thing with named incompatibilities.
- *(M) Cross-system caution:* the documented interaction with edema/hypertension (below) functions as a _de facto_ conditional viruddha and should be flagged, even though the saṃhitās frame it only as a Kapha/_śotha_ caution.#footnote[Kapha / _śotha_ / _medo-roga_ contraindications: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_, and general dravyaguna principle. The (M) cross-system caution is a modern addition keyed to the 11β-HSD2 / mineralocorticoid effect — not classical — and is flagged accordingly.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Licorice is one of the most-praised drugs in Tibb — the sweet root that the tradition calls_ aṣl al-sūs _("root of licorice") and treats as one of the foundational pulmonary and digestive tonics. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* عرق السوس (_ʿirq al-sūs_, "vein of licorice") or أصل السوس (_aṣl al-sūs_, "root of licorice"); سوس (_sūs_) alone. Persian: شیرین بیان (_shirin bayān_, "sweet root"). Urdu/Hakeemi: ملٹھی (_mulhaṭṭī_, from Sanskrit _madhuyaṣṭī_) and اصل السوس (_aṣl al-sūs_).

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _ʿirq al-sūs_). Diverges from the Ayurvedic _śīta vīrya_ (cooling) — another standing cross-system disagreement. The Tibb Hot-Moist classification underlies the warming-and-moistening pulmonary use.

*Af'āl:* *Mulayyin* (gentle laxative — sweet-moist register); *muqawwī al-ṣadr* (chest tonic — the principal pulmonary action); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *munaffith* (expectorant); *mughayyir li-l-balgham al-yābis* (modifies dry phlegm — for dry cough); *muḥsin li-l-ṣawt* (voice-improving — the classical Greco-Arabic-Indian convergent use); *jālī* (cleansing); *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī*; *mukhālif li-l-sumūm* (anti-toxin — the classical Tibb antidote register, paralleling the Ayurvedic _viṣa-ghna_ use); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-improving).

*Istemālāt:* dry cough; bronchitis; chronic respiratory weakness; hoarseness; throat ulcers; chronic gastric pain and ulcer (the modern DGL indication has classical precedent in this register); chronic constipation of dry pattern; mild toxicity; thirst; debility.

*Mawāne':* edema (*istisqā'*); hypertension; the documented 11β-HSD2 / mineralocorticoid effect was unknown classically but the *amrāḍ al-isqā' wa-l-ḥaqn* (dropsy / fluid-retention disease) caution is the classical equivalent — appearing in Tibb texts as a precaution against heavy prolonged use; pregnancy at high dose.

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; rose-water; warming spices for the phlegmatic constitution.

*Badal:* unrefined cane sugar (*sukkar*); date syrup.

*Tibb formulas:* *Joshānda-yi Sūs* (licorice decoction — the basic preparation); *Sharbat-i Sūs* (licorice syrup — the canonical Unani cough syrup, paralleling the Brown Mixture / Pulvis Liquiritiae Compositus tradition); *Sufūf-i Sūs* (licorice powder); *Maʿjūn-i Sūs* and many compound electuaries; *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd Khās*, *Khamīrah-i Abresham Sāda*, and Mufarriḥ-class cardiacs use licorice as moderating and harmonizing ingredient (the Unani parallel to the Chinese 調和諸藥 role); the famous Egyptian *ʿirq sūs* beverage (a chilled licorice decoction, sold in Cairo streets in summer) is a Maghrebi-Egyptian folk continuation of the classical Tibb register.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _ʿirq al-sūs_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _mulhaṭṭī / aṣl al-sūs_ entry.#footnote[Tibb sūs: Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār; the Hot-Moist Tibb classification diverges from the Ayurvedic cooling reading.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *炙甘草湯 (Zhì Gāncǎo Tāng, "Honey-fried Licorice Decoction")* — also called 復脈湯 (Fùmài Tāng, "Restore the Pulse Decoction"); from the _Shanghan Lun_. Honey-fried licorice is the chief herb; for Heart qi-and-blood vacuity with palpitations and an irregular/intermittent pulse (knotted or regularly-interrupted pulse). A foundational arrhythmia formula.
- *芍藥甘草湯 (Sháoyào Gāncǎo Tāng, "Peony and Licorice Decoction")* — from the _Shanghan Lun_; just two herbs, white peony + honey-fried licorice, to relax cramping and spasm (calf cramps, abdominal spasmodic pain) by "harmonizing the Liver and relaxing tension."
- *四逆湯 (Sìnì Tāng, "Frigid Extremities Decoction")* — from the _Shanghan Lun_; aconite + dried ginger + honey-fried licorice. Here licorice both tonifies and *moderates the toxicity of aconite* — the classic illustration of its harmonizing/detoxifying role.
- *甘草瀉心湯 / 半夏瀉心湯 family (Gāncǎo / Bànxià Xièxīn Tāng)* — "draining the heart" formulas for epigastric focal distention, where licorice harmonizes the cold–hot herb pairing.
- *桂枝湯 (Guìzhī Tāng, "Cinnamon Twig Decoction")* — the archetypal exterior-harmonizing formula; licorice is a standard supporting/harmonizing ingredient, illustrating its near-ubiquitous "escort" function.
- *甘麥大棗湯 (Gān Mài Dà Zǎo Tāng, "Licorice, Wheat & Jujube Decoction")* — from the _Jīnguì Yàolüè_; for "restless organ disorder" (_zàngzào_, an emotional/agitation pattern), licorice as chief with wheat and jujube.
- *Raw vs. prepared:* raw 生甘草 is used for clearing heat/toxicity and throat; honey-fried 炙甘草 for tonifying qi and the middle. Typical decoction dose 2–10 g, usually as a minor harmonizing member at 1–3 g.#footnote[Formula sources: Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_ (傷寒論) and _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略); compositions, indications, and the raw/honey-fried distinction follow Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009), and Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Yaṣṭimadhu cūrṇa* — the simple powder, taken with honey or ghee (commonly ~1–3 g) for cough, hoarseness, hyperacidity, and as a _medhya_/_rasāyana_.
- *Yaṣṭyādi / Eladi-type formulations* — yaṣṭimadhu appears across classical compound groups; it is a member of Caraka's _jīvanīya gaṇa_ (the ten life-promoting herbs) and _sandhānīya_ (tissue-uniting) and _kaṇṭhya_ (throat-benefiting) groups.
- *Yaṣṭimadhu taila / ghṛta* — medicated oil and ghee preparations: *Yaṣṭimadhu taila* for external/wound and hair use (_keśya_, _vraṇa-ropaṇa_); medicated ghṛta for Pitta and internal demulcent use.
- *Eladi vati / Khadiradi-type lozenges* — throat/cough troches frequently include yaṣṭimadhu.
- *In _rasāyana_ and eye/skin preparations* — used in _varṇya_ (complexion) lepas and _cakṣuṣya_ eye formulations; combined with milk as a _kṣīrapāka_ (milk decoction) for rejuvenative and Pitta-pacifying use.
- *In _vamana_ (therapeutic emesis)* — yaṣṭimadhu decoction (_yaṣṭimadhu phāṇṭa_) is a classical emetic medium in _pañcakarma_.#footnote[Formulation sources: Caraka Saṃhitā (Sūtrasthāna 4 _jīvanīya/sandhānīya/kaṇṭhya gaṇa_; Kalpasthāna and Siddhisthāna for _vamana_); Bhāvaprakāśa and standard Ayurvedic formularies (e.g. _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ for _cūrṇa_/_taila_/_ghṛta_ preparation methods; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_ for compound recipes). Translations: Sharma & Dash; Murthy, _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* _Theophrastus_ (4th c. BCE) records "Scythian root" (_glykeîa rhíza_) held in the mouth to endure thirst; _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_, 1st c. CE) prescribes _glykyrrhiza_ decoction for the throat, chest, cough, stomach/"heartburn," thirst, and kidney/bladder complaints; _Pliny_ likewise for voice, throat, and thirst.
- *Medieval / monastic:* carried through Galen into medieval herbalism; cultivated in European monastic _officina_ gardens (cf. _officinalis_, "of the storeroom") and notably at Pontefract, Yorkshire, where licorice lozenges ("Pontefract cakes") originated — the lineage from medicine to confectionery.
- *Classical galenic preparations:* _Succus liquiritiae_ (licorice extract/juice, the hard black "Spanish juice" sticks), _Extractum glycyrrhizae_, and licorice-based lozenges/pastilles (_trochisci_) and cough linctuses; long an official _Pharmacopoeia_ ingredient as a demulcent, expectorant, and flavoring/masking agent.
- *Modern pharmaceutical survivals:* *DGL (deglycyrrhizinated licorice)* for peptic ulcer/reflux (glycyrrhizin removed to avoid the blood-pressure effect); *carbenoxolone*, a semi-synthetic glycyrrhetinic-acid derivative, was used as an anti-ulcer drug; licorice extract persists as a pharmaceutical flavoring and as a cough-preparation ingredient.#footnote[Greco-Roman uses: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_. Galenic preparations and pharmacopoeial history: standard pharmacognosy/pharmacy history references (e.g. Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_). Carbenoxolone and DGL: clinical-pharmacology literature — verify against current sources.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Licorice root is a medicinal, not a food nutrient source. The pharmacology here carries a real safety signal.

*Key constituents:*
- *Glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizic acid)* — the sweet triterpenoid saponin, ~30–50× sweeter than sucrose; the molecular basis of the "sweet root" name across all three languages. Hydrolyzes to *glycyrrhetinic acid (enoxolone)*, the active aglycone.
- *Flavonoids* — liquiritin, liquiritigenin, isoliquiritigenin; *glabridin* (in _G. glabra_).
- Polysaccharides, coumarins.#footnote[Phytochemistry of _Glycyrrhiza_: standard pharmacognosy references (e.g. Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_) and current reviews — verify against current literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *The mineralocorticoid effect — the central safety issue.* Glycyrrhetinic acid inhibits *11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11β-HSD2)*, the enzyme that normally inactivates cortisol in the kidney. With the enzyme blocked, cortisol acts on renal mineralocorticoid receptors like aldosterone — producing sodium/water retention, potassium loss, hypertension, and a hypokalemic, hypertensive state ("apparent mineralocorticoid excess"). This maps with precision onto the classical cautions — TCM's 中滿 / water-retention warning and the Ayurvedic _śotha_ / Kapha / _kledādhikya_ contraindication are, mechanistically, the 11β-HSD2 / sodium-retention effect, described centuries before the enzyme was known.#footnote[The 11β-HSD2 inhibition mechanism and "apparent mineralocorticoid excess" from glycyrrhizin/glycyrrhetinic acid are well established in clinical pharmacology and toxicology; documented cases of licorice-induced hypertension and hypokalemia from confectionery and tea exist. Verify against current reviews (clinical-toxicology and endocrinology journals / EFSA opinions).]
- *Anti-inflammatory / mucosal:* glycyrrhetinic acid has corticosteroid-like and demulcent activity; *DGL* is used for peptic ulcer/reflux to keep the gastric-protective effect without the blood-pressure risk. This validates the _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ and 清熱解毒/throat uses.
- *Antitussive / expectorant:* supports the cross-traditional cough/throat use (止咳, _kāsahara/svarya_).
- *Antiviral / antimicrobial:* glycyrrhizin shows in-vitro antiviral activity; clinical relevance still under study.
- *Endocrine:* can mildly lower testosterone; estrogenic flavonoids (glabridin) — relevant in pregnancy and hormone-sensitive conditions.
- *Herb–drug interactions (flag prominently):* potentiates potassium loss with thiazide/loop diuretics and risks digoxin toxicity via hypokalemia; opposes antihypertensives; interacts with corticosteroids.#footnote[DGL for peptic ulcer, antitussive/antiviral activity, endocrine effects, and the diuretic/digoxin/antihypertensive interactions are documented in pharmacology and herb–drug-interaction references; verify against current literature, as the clinical and regulatory picture is active.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* The relevant figure is *glycyrrhizin*, not raw root weight (glycyrrhizin is ~2–9% of dried root, highly variable).
- *WHO / JECFA:* provisional acceptable intake ~2 mg glycyrrhizin/kg body weight/day (≈ 100–150 mg/day for an average adult) — the most-cited upper reference.
- *EU Scientific Committee on Food (1991):* suggested ceiling ~100 mg glycyrrhizin/day for regular consumption; effects appear reliably above ~200–400 mg/day.
- *Harm thresholds:* sustained intake >~100 mg/day can raise blood pressure / lower potassium in sensitive people; >~400 mg/day reliably produces mineralocorticoid effects over weeks. Toxicity is cumulative and duration-dependent.
- *Traditional dosing context:* Ayurvedic _yaṣṭimadhu_ powder ~1–3 g/day; TCM 甘草 in decoction ~2–10 g/day, usually as a minor harmonizing ingredient (1–3 g) in a multi-herb formula — traditional practice implicitly stayed under the modern thresholds.
- *Risk groups (stay well below, or avoid):* hypertension, heart disease, kidney disease, low potassium; those on diuretics, digoxin, corticosteroids, antihypertensives; pregnancy. *DGL* sidesteps the glycyrrhizin risk entirely.#footnote[Dosage thresholds: WHO/JECFA provisional ADI (~2 mg glycyrrhizic acid/kg/day) and the EU Scientific Committee on Food opinion on glycyrrhizinic acid (1991, reaffirmed by EFSA). Traditional dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (甘草), and standard Ayurvedic dravyaguna texts (_yaṣṭimadhu_). These are reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice — verify against the latest JECFA/EFSA publications, as regulatory limits are revised. Anyone using licorice therapeutically, especially in the risk groups, should consult a clinician.]

